.\" Manual page for emojitron
.Dd September 7, 2026
.Dt EMOJITRON 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm emojitron
.Nd print random emoji, or emoji from a standard group
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nu
.Op Fl g Ar group
.Op Fl s Ar separator
.Op Fl t Ar tone
.Op Fl -seed Ar seed
.Op Ar count
.Nm
.Fl l
.Nm
.Op Fl hV
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes emoji to standard output.
With no options it prints a single emoji drawn uniformly at random from the
whole Unicode emoji set.
Randomness comes from the operating system's cryptographically secure random
number generator, so the output is not predictable or reproducible unless
.Fl -seed
is given.
.Pp
The optional
.Ar count
argument says how many emoji to print; it defaults to 1.
A count of 0 prints nothing and warns on standard error.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl g , Fl -group Ar group
Draw only from the named emoji group.
See
.Sx GROUPS .
An unrecognized name is an error, and
.Nm
suggests the closest match.
.It Fl l , Fl -list-groups
List the group names, the number of emoji in each, and the corresponding CLDR
group, then exit.
This option may not be combined with any other.
.It Fl n , Fl -names
Print the CLDR name and the
.Ql :shortcode:\&
after each emoji.
Emoji without a shortcode show
.Ql :-:\& .
Implies a newline separator unless
.Fl s
is given.
.It Fl s , Fl -separator Ar separator
Print
.Ar separator
between emoji instead of the default, which is a single space, or a newline
when
.Fl n
is in effect.
A trailing newline is always written after the last emoji.
.It Fl -seed Ar seed
Seed the generator with the unsigned 64-bit integer
.Ar seed .
The same seed, count, group and options produce the same emoji every run,
which is useful for tests and for reproducible output in scripts.
Seeded output is
.Em not
unpredictable: anyone who knows the seed knows the output.
The sequence is stable within a release, but a future version of
.Nm
or of its emoji tables may reorder the pool and change what a given seed
produces.
.It Fl t , Fl -skin-tone Ar tone
Apply
.Ar tone
to every printed emoji that supports a skin tone modifier.
Accepted tones are the five Fitzpatrick modifiers
.Cm light , medium-light , medium , medium-dark
and
.Cm dark ,
plus
.Cm default
for the unmodified yellow form.
Emoji with no skin tone, and multi-person sequences that take one tone per
person, are printed unchanged; if that is true of every emoji printed,
.Nm
says so on standard error.
.It Fl u , Fl -unique
Never print the same emoji twice.
If
.Ar count
exceeds the number of emoji available in the pool,
.Nm
warns on standard error and prints the whole pool instead.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a help summary
.Pq Fl h
or the full help text
.Pq Fl -help
and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh GROUPS
The group names correspond to the standard Unicode CLDR emoji groups defined by
Unicode Technical Standard #51.
Each name also accepts its full hyphenated CLDR spelling, shown in parentheses.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "activities" -compact
.It Cm smileys
Smileys & Emotion
.Pq Cm smileys-and-emotion
.It Cm people
People & Body
.Pq Cm people-and-body
.It Cm animals
Animals & Nature
.Pq Cm animals-and-nature
.It Cm food
Food & Drink
.Pq Cm food-and-drink
.It Cm travel
Travel & Places
.Pq Cm travel-and-places
.It Cm activities
Activities
.It Cm objects
Objects
.It Cm symbols
Symbols
.It Cm flags
Flags
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Nm
exits 2 when the command line cannot be parsed.
A warning about a clamped or zero count is not an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print one random emoji:
.Pp
.Dl $ emojitron
.Pp
Print ten random emoji separated by spaces:
.Pp
.Dl $ emojitron 10
.Pp
Print five distinct food emoji with no separator:
.Pp
.Dl $ emojitron -g food -u -s '' 5
.Pp
Print four emoji with a medium-dark skin tone:
.Pp
.Dl $ emojitron -t medium-dark 4
.Pp
Print the same five emoji every time:
.Pp
.Dl $ emojitron --seed 42 5
.Pp
List three animals with their names and shortcodes:
.Pp
.Dl $ emojitron -n -g animals 3
.Pp
Show which groups exist and how large they are:
.Pp
.Dl $ emojitron --list-groups
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Lk https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr51/ "Unicode Technical Standard #51, Unicode Emoji"
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Brian Shumate
.Sh CAVEATS
Rendering depends entirely on the terminal and its fonts.
Emoji built from ZWJ sequences, flags, and recent additions may appear as
several glyphs, as a box, or misaligned in columns even though the bytes
written are correct.
